          "message": {
            "text": "Rejecting malformed ZIP headers can allocate gigabytes of diagnostic text\n\nMalformed ZIP entries can repeatedly reference one long local-header name. The structural checker copies that name into every retained diagnostic before production callers take the first error, allowing a small rejected archive to exhaust memory.\n\nSeverity: low\n\nCategory: Resource amplification during archive refusal\n\nWeaknesses: CWE-400\n\nRemediation:\nShort-circuit structural validation for production readers; separately bound individual diagnostic names and aggregate debug diagnostic bytes, and avoid copying shared malformed header data repeatedly.\n\nRemediation tests:\n- Use a small configured diagnostic budget and repeated local-header references to assert bounded refusal.\n- Keep debug reporting useful while truncating repeated long names and reporting truncation."
          },
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "codexSecurity/v1": "codex-security/v1:sha256:26a86e2b0a1392bf7b2b74208b822a783361165e4bd4fa9b60d325c515abdce0"
