          "message": {
            "text": "Cyclic Markdown drawing dependencies terminate conversion through unbounded recursion\n\nA drawing: image destination in ordinary Markdown is base64-decoded and deserialized into a dependency graph without validation. Package materialization recursively follows each dep.rels.part before writing the dependent part and has no active-node or completed-node map. A one-node self-reference causes indefinite recursion and stack exhaustion, even with default Markdown conversion options and no image loader. Header/footer drawing payloads also reach this same materializer through view_stories::story_part. Capture memoizes a part before descending, so even capture-produced envelopes can encode a cycle; capture count/byte limits do not establish an acyclic imported graph. The supplied discovery-0030 trace additionally records that an empty boxes collection bypasses text-box-specific Writer finish checks; these checks do not validate the dependency graph.\n\nSeverity: medium\n\nCategory: Uncontrolled dependency graph recursion Uncontrolled recursive dependency expansion\n\nWeaknesses: CWE-674\n\nRemediation:\nValidate dependency indices, count/byte/depth limits and graph shape at import. Materialize with an active/completed traversal state and index-to-output map, rejecting cycles or reserving identities to support cycles without recursive expansion. Memoize shared subgraphs. Enforce explicit dependency-edge and traversal-work budgets before assembly; when rejecting active-node revisits return a typed conversion refusal. Apply the same graph validation and traversal state to header/footer drawing materialization, not only body drawings.\n\nRemediation tests:\n- Use one-node and two-node cyclic dependency graphs and assert refusal or bounded supported materialization.\n- Test repeated acyclic shared dependencies and import count/depth limits.\n- Convert a self-cycle and a two-node cycle and assert a typed refusal without recursion.\n- Convert a deeply nested chain and a shared-dependency DAG under bounded work and ensure valid parts retain expected identity.\n- Assert a self-cycle and a two-node cycle return a typed refusal without stack overflow.\n- Assert a shared acyclic dependency materializes once and preserves relationships.\n- Apply decode-side dependency/edge/byte budgets to external Markdown envelopes.\n- Reject a self-reference and a two-node dependency cycle through public Markdown conversion.\n- Verify shared acyclic dependencies materialize once and valid captured drawings round-trip.\n- Self-cycles and two-node cycles in valid base64 Drawing payloads must return a typed error without recursion.\n- A bounded shared-child DAG should reconstruct each node once and ordinary captured drawings should round-trip.\n- Reject a one-node self-cycle and a two-node cycle without aborting the process.\n- Preserve shared acyclic dependencies and reject over-budget dependency graphs.\n- Cover one-node self-cycles, multi-node cycles, deep chains and repeated DAG edges.\n- Assert either bounded rejection or correct shared-part materialization without process termination.\n- Convert an inline drawing envelope with a self-cycle and assert a bounded typed refusal.\n- Cover a two-node cycle, deep acyclic chain, repeated shared dependency and over-budget graph.\n- Import a self-referential dependency and a two-node cycle and assert bounded refusal or safe graph-preserving completion.\n- Import a shared acyclic dependency graph and assert each part is emitted once.\n- Reject self-cycles and multi-node cycles with a typed bounded error.\n- Import a shared acyclic dependency graph once per node and preserve legitimate drawing behavior.\n\nPreventive controls:\n- Treat integer-indexed serialized graphs as untrusted graphs rather than assuming capture-produced invariants."
          },
