          "message": {
            "text": "Rendering an HTML chunk can allocate gigabytes from a single colspan value\n\nA document author can place a huge positive colspan in a small embedded HTML/MHT altChunk table. Ordinary DOCX rendering generates one grid XML element per effective column after original package admission and before DOM/layout checks. A count of 1000000000 drives a billion appends; 4294967295 generates over 85 GB of markup, exhausting CPU/memory.\n\nSeverity: medium\n\nCategory: Uncontrolled resource allocation Unbounded allocation from HTML numeric input Unbounded generated markup expansion\n\nWeaknesses: CWE-789, CWE-400, CWE-770, CWE-834\n\nRemediation:\nEnforce a practical maximum cell span and checked per-row/aggregate column counts before generating any table XML. Charge all HTML/MHT-generated WML, including repeated/nested tables, to a cumulative byte/work budget inside altChunk expansion and return a typed input-limit refusal before allocation or DOM construction.\n\nRemediation tests:\n- A tiny HTML chunk with colspan=1000000000 must refuse before allocation.\n- Check multi-cell aggregate span overflow and repeated/nested table expansion budgets.\n- Excessive individual/cumulative colspan in HTML and MHT must fail with bounded output/work.\n- Cover malformed spans, overflow and nested tables.\n- Preserve valid small HTML tables.\n- Unit tests with a small configured column cap cover the inclusive limit, one above it, oversized numeric values, and sums of several otherwise valid spans.\n- Integration test through a DOCX altChunk confirms refusal occurs before grid generation.\n- Use a tiny in-memory altChunk HTML with excessive td/th colspan and assert a prompt typed refusal without output growth.\n- Exercise cumulative spans across multiple cells, numeric overflow and valid inclusive limits.\n- A tiny altChunk with colspan 1000000000 must fail before large output allocation.\n- Cover inclusive limits, cumulative spans across cells, integer overflow, nested tables, and both HTML and MHT import.\n- Reject an oversized colspan in a small admitted DOCX without allocating proportional output.\n- Cover aggregate spans across multiple cells and arithmetic overflow while preserving normal merged tables.\n- A one-cell HTML table with an excessive numeric colspan returns a typed refusal without allocating proportional output.\n- Multiple spans cannot overflow the cumulative count or bypass the generated-grid limit.\n- Ordinary bounded colspan tables retain rendering behavior.\n- Reject huge representable positive colspan through public rendering while retaining ordinary span behavior.\n- Verify many bounded spans cannot exceed the cumulative table/output budget.\n- Verify row-span sum overflow returns a refusal on native and wasm32.\n\nPreventive controls:\n- Charge representation expansion to explicit output/work budgets rather than relying only on compressed/inflated input limits.\n- Budget derived representations before materialization, including post-admission HTML/MHT expansion."
          },
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "codexSecurity/v1": "codex-security/v1:sha256:4b8f02dccd426675bf3a21de95fa4991dcb3f5b38706bb1f1cc6703835b1dbf2"
          },
