          ],
          "message": {
            "text": "Opaque DTD and top-level CDATA bypass package XML depth checks\n\nPackage admission ignores opaque DTD and top-level CDATA events, while the permissive DOM handles top-level <! constructs by skipping only to the first '>'. A delimiter within a quoted entity or CDATA body exposes deeply nested literal markup as recursive elements, bypassing admission depth and potentially exhausting the host stack. Rendering and lazy WmlDocument main-part access are preserved affected entrypoints.\nAn internal-subset comment with a literal > supplies an additional hidden-tag framing at the same declaration boundary. The assigned corroboration additionally identifies get_revisions main-document parsing after ordinary admission.\n\nSeverity: medium\n\nCategory: Uncontrolled recursion from XML parser disagreement XML grammar differential bypassing recursion policy XML admission parser differential\n\nWeaknesses: CWE-674\n\nRemediation:\nAlign admission and DOM lexical/structural semantics for every opaque <! construct. Reject unsupported DTD and CDATA/text outside the root consistently with checked XML validation, and enforce active depth during DOM construction or use the checked API before every untrusted consumer. If opaque constructs are supported, consume their complete grammar rather than the first '>'. A DTD-only refusal does not fix the top-level CDATA subcase; retain valid in-root CDATA. Include get_revisions main-document parsing in the mandatory lexical/depth boundary while preserving existing checked-consumer distinctions.\n\nRemediation tests:\n- Package XML containing an unused quoted DTD literal with tag text must refuse before DOM construction.\n- Compare admission and checked-parser outcomes for DTD/internal-subset/comment/quote cases.\n- Preserve supported ordinary XML documents and depth-limit boundaries.\n- Regression input with a quoted internal entity value containing nested tags must be rejected before DOM construction.\n- Cover the package admission and WmlDocument main-document path with a small configured depth limit.\n- Check deeply nested markup inside quoted DTD entities and top-level CDATA; admission/public document APIs must refuse before DOM recursion.\n- Retain valid in-root CDATA and ordinary XML-depth boundary cases.\n- A DOCTYPE internal-subset comment containing > and deeply nested-looking tags must refuse before DOM recursion.\n- Cover quoted > and internal subset comment/PI variants through WmlDocument and render/compare consumers.\n- Assert package admission rejects a DOCTYPE internal subset containing quoted markup before any DOM work.\n- Verify checked raw XML and package conversion agree on declaration refusals.\n- Cover both normal XML extensions and relationship-selected auxiliary inputs without weakening existing tolerances.\n- Add deterministic regression inputs for the source-supported differential and assert a typed bounded refusal.\n- Verify ordinary compatible documents still process under unchanged limits.\n- Refuse a DTD carrying quoted markup before any DOM construction in admitted public parser wrappers.\n- Verify actual recursive DOM depth cannot exceed the configured limit even if lexical prevalidation differs.\n- Deterministic boundary regression tests should assert typed refusal or bounded work for the identified tokenizer/classification/count discrepancy, with normal supported inputs preserved.\n- Deep tag text inside an unused DTD entity returns INVALID_XML before DOM construction.\n- Checked and package admission APIs consistently reject DTD declarations.\n- Ordinary within-limit XML remains accepted.\n- Refuse quoted-DTD markup without recursive parsing through package/revision APIs.\n- Verify admitted depth limits remain effective across declarations and all downstream XML parsers.\n- Assert package admission rejects a DTD containing deeply nested literal markup in an entity value.\n- Cover greater-than signs inside quoted declaration content and ensure admission/DOM interpretation stays consistent."
          },
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "codexSecurity/v1": "codex-security/v1:sha256:24785c7e899acb7c71a4d83208d83bc2e7d5a8ba351fc3e77418b1a43b20e871"
          },
