          "message": {
            "text": "Ragged legacy DOC tables expand into an unbounded padded rectangle\n\nA table with one wide row and many narrow rows contains relatively few cells, but legacy Markdown serialization emits the widest row's cell count for every row. Compact input can therefore generate gigabytes of output and exhaust importer memory. The same unbounded serialization is independently exposed through public to_markdown(LegacyDocument), in addition to DOC byte conversions. Assigned discovery-0031 supplies a static 100000-cell row plus 100000 short/empty rows framing: about ten billion serialized cells before DOCX admission. No numeric colspan forgery or logical-piece alias is required.\n\nSeverity: medium\n\nCategory: Allocation without resource limits\n\nWeaknesses: CWE-770, CWE-409\n\nRemediation:\nBefore rectangularizing a table, use checked multiplication to enforce a generated-cell quota and enforce cumulative output-byte/work limits. Refuse over-budget tables with a typed error. Enforce a supported column ceiling and cover doc_to_markdown and doc_to_docx's Markdown intermediate; a bounded sparse representation is another option.\n\nRemediation tests:\n- A wide row followed by many narrow rows must refuse before padded output growth exceeds quota.\n- Small ragged tables and generated-cell quota boundaries must retain expected Markdown/DOCX behavior.\n- Assert adversarial small-input amplification is refused within active resource limits.\n- Cover the inclusive accepted limits and preserve legitimate document behavior.\n- Add deterministic bounded-input regression coverage around the broken control; for filesystem staging use isolated integration cases with pre-existing symlink entries and verify unrelated files remain unchanged.\n- Use a small deterministic document with extreme semantic values or alternate valid OPC paths and assert controlled refusal or complete selected sanitization before expensive processing.\n- A wide row followed by many short/empty rows must be refused before output proportional to rows*width is materialized."
          },
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "codexSecurity/v1": "codex-security/v1:sha256:df4f27aeba00deaedb340492f8977d43f43a1d23716a9213f9ea14a1a5d32867"
          },
          "properties": {
            "category": "Allocation without resource limits",
            "confidence": "high",
