          ],
          "message": {
            "text": "Release script exposes the PyPI publishing token in process arguments\n\nThe release operator exports UV_PUBLISH_TOKEN, which preflight checks without printing. Step 11 then expands that credential into `uv publish --token \"$UV_PUBLISH_TOKEN\" dist/pypi/*`. The spawned uv process therefore contains the token in argv, exposing it through process listings or command-line telemetry on hosts permitting those observations. Disclosure of a PyPI publishing credential. An observer could use the captured token to publish packages within its granted scope until expiration or revocation. Its exact scope and lifetime are not established by the source. The assigned audit leaves uv argument-scrubbing timing and host process permissions unverified, yielding a medium-confidence assessment retained alongside the established high-confidence source-flow assessment.\n\nSeverity: low\n\nCategory: Sensitive credentials in process arguments\n\nWeaknesses: CWE-214\n\nRemediation:\nRemove the --token argument and let uv obtain the already exported UV_PUBLISH_TOKEN through its environment-variable credential mechanism. Keep credential values out of process arguments and release logs.\n\nRemediation tests:\n- Use a deterministic command-builder test with a dummy token to assert argv never contains credential material while credential injection remains environment-based.\n- Verify the release command contains no expanded credential argument while retaining UV_PUBLISH_TOKEN-based authentication.\n\nPreventive controls:\n- Review release-tool invocations for secrets moved from private stores/environment into argv."
          },
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "codexSecurity/v1": "codex-security/v1:sha256:bae0511524c7601c85d30654b37a2b6027c23f7405f99a43572c1ac24dfcd88f"
