          "message": {
            "text": "Large Chicago numbering values can exhaust memory while reading or rendering a DOCX\n\nAn untrusted DOCX can select Chicago numbering with a huge list start/startOverride or section page start. The shared chicago_label formatter repeats a UTF-8 symbol roughly n/4 times without a length bound, allowing gigabyte allocations during list rendering, ordinary section-page processing and Markdown numbered-list views. Authored endnote starts also reach this shared formatter. The supplied u32::MAX framing selects 1073741824 double-daggers, about 3 GiB of UTF-8 label bytes; this is static arithmetic. Additional supplied static framing: start=2147483647 selects 536870912 UTF-8 double-daggers (\u2021), requesting 1.5 GiB of marker bytes before downstream copies.\n Assigned discovery-0032 additionally supplies start=4294967294: 1073741824 daggers (\u2020), about 3 GiB of UTF-8 before copies. Level-zero reader/agent labels reach the shared formatter without a large ilvl.\n\nSeverity: medium\n\nCategory: Uncontrolled allocation in numeric label expansion Unbounded allocation in number label formatting\n\nWeaknesses: CWE-789\n\nRemediation:\nBound generated byte/glyph length inside the shared Chicago formatter before repetition, with a documented safe cycle/fallback or typed refusal. Apply that bound to all list, startOverride, section-page and Markdown/direct list_number callers; do not rely on input bytes, counter saturation or later clipping. Include authored endnote numStart/format and MCP agent-view callers in the shared formatter budget.\nAdditional retained remediation detail: Apply a central maximum output length to Chicago labels before repetition and enforce a cumulative numbering-output budget. Use a documented bounded fallback or typed refusal consistently for list, read/agent, page and future formatter consumers.\n\nRemediation tests:\n- Cover maximum u32 Chicago start and startOverride values without large allocations.\n- Exercise section page labels and Markdown list-label views as well as ordinary list rendering.\n- Reject or safely cycle a Chicago list start/startOverride at u32::MAX.\n- Exercise Chicago section pgNumType start=u32::MAX on an ordinary paragraph and direct list_number callers.\n- Chicago starts near u32::MAX must avoid proportional allocation in render and Markdown output.\n- Verify inclusive label budgets and preserve existing alphabetic/Roman wrapping.\n- Chicago values near u32::MAX must return a bounded result or typed resource-limit error without large allocation.\n- Exercise ordinary list labels, section page labels and DOCX-to-Markdown list markers through the same fixed helper.\n- Refuse oversized Chicago starts and overrides at level zero before allocating a large label.\n- Exercise the same malformed counter through rendering and agent-view reading, and retain ordinary Chicago cycles.\n- Maximum u32 start and startOverride never allocate proportional marker text.\n- Normal Chicago cycles preserve their expected markers.\n- Reject huge Chicago list starts and overrides through reading and rendering APIs.\n- Cover page and endnote Chicago starts as sibling routes.\n- Keep ordinary Chicago symbol cycles and existing Roman/alphabetic behavior.\n- Use small test label budgets to verify exact-boundary and over-limit refusal from list starts, startOverride, page starts, endnote starts and Markdown reads.\n- Verify large starts in bounded numeric formats remain supported without proportional allocation.\n- Use small deterministic boundary cases for Chicago label byte limits, huge start/startOverride values and normal Chicago sequences; assert refusal occurs before repetition.\n- Use a small deterministic document with extreme semantic values or alternate valid OPC paths and assert controlled refusal or complete selected sanitization before expensive processing.\n- Read and render level-zero Chicago lists using large start and startOverride values; assert bounded refusal/fallback.\n- Exercise Chicago page numbering with a large section start, and preserve small intended labels in reading and rendering.\n\nPreventive controls:\n- Bound derived dimensions and generated output independently from archive/XML input byte limits.\n- Every numeric-to-text formatter should have an explicit output-size bound."
          },
