          ],
          "message": {
            "text": "Document numbering values allocate multi-gigabyte Chicago labels\n\nA small admitted DOCX can set a list start or endnote numStart to 4294967295 with Chicago numbering. The renderer converts that numeric value directly into 1,073,741,824 repeated double-daggers, allocating 3 GiB before subsequent layout and copies. Document suppliers can exhaust a consuming process without a large archive or deep XML.\n\nSeverity: medium\n\nCategory: Uncontrolled memory allocation\n\nWeaknesses: CWE-789\n\nRemediation:\nEnforce a finite expanded-marker byte budget before allocation for every numeric-format consumer. Refuse or safely fall back when start/counter values exceed format-specific limits; propagate a typed refusal instead of allocating. Cover list starts, startOverride, and endnote numbering.\n\nRemediation tests:\n- Use deterministic formatter tests at the maximum accepted marker size and just beyond it, including u32::MAX, without allocating the oversized string.\n- Add integration fixtures with small Chicago list starts/startOverride and endnote numStart that exceed the output budget; assert bounded refusal."
          },
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "codexSecurity/v1": "codex-security/v1:sha256:e703dec23fa5c86a79d8d1e702f3afb48186eb2325a55144f0f33f47046c8f5a"
          },
