          ],
          "message": {
            "text": "Rendering an admitted DOCX can retain gigabytes of duplicate embedded fonts\n\nDistinct font-table aliases may all reference one embedded font part. Rendering eagerly loads every alias, copies the full font and retains a separate buffer for each name, multiplying a small admitted package into gigabytes of memory even if its body never uses those fonts.\n\nSeverity: medium\n\nCategory: Unbounded expansion of shared embedded resources\n\nWeaknesses: CWE-409, CWE-400, CWE-789\n\nRemediation:\nCache decoded font buffers by resolved package part and effective obfuscation key, share Arc values across aliases, and enforce an aggregate decoded-font allocation/work budget before copying.\nEnforce face-count limits before decoding/retention and load only used faces where possible; cover all four style slots and different effective keys.\n\nRemediation tests:\n- Load many unique aliases referencing one valid embedded part and assert shared buffers and bounded aggregate allocation.\n- Exercise distinct effective font keys and malformed fonts without bypassing aggregate budgets.\n- Many aliases referencing one font/key must share decoded backing storage or remain within an aggregate budget.\n- Cover distinct keys, four styles, unused aliases, catalogue skips, and malformed fonts.\n- Many distinct noncatalogue aliases referencing one valid part must share backing storage or return a typed resource-limit refusal under a small configured budget.\n- Many aliases of one part share one decoded payload.\n- Distinct embedded fonts collectively respect a retained-byte budget.\n- Unused font definitions do not trigger unnecessary whole-font copies.\n- Reference one large font from many aliases and verify storage is shared or a typed budget refusal occurs.\n- Preserve alias/style resolution and plain/obfuscated font support.\n- Add deterministic bounded-input regression coverage around the broken control; for filesystem staging use isolated integration cases with pre-existing symlink entries and verify unrelated files remain unchanged.\n- Many aliases to one part share a single allocation or refuse at a fixed aggregate budget.\n- Distinct deobfuscation keys remain distinct; malformed font programs still refuse."
          },
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "codexSecurity/v1": "codex-security/v1:sha256:831d2ee391c9515019b410eaa5cf0a1dc256e7434976da406653290b67b2284d"
          },
          "properties": {
            "category": "Unbounded expansion of shared embedded resources",
