          "message": {
            "text": "Aliased legacy DOC pieces expand small physical text into enormous logical stories\n\nThousands of contiguous logical pieces can all reference the same physical text range. For example, 4095 pieces referencing one common 1 MiB compressed-text range describe nearly 4 GiB of logical characters while requiring only roughly 49 KiB of piece records. main_text materializes metadata for every character, requiring tens of GiB before rendering. A second source-derived framing uses approximately 65,535 pieces reusing 64 KiB, producing over four billion logical characters from a piece table below 1 MiB; expansion precedes later auxiliary-table checks. The assigned 1024-by-65536 framing yields 67108864 logical characters and at least 768 MiB for char/CP/FC fields alone, before modifiers and allocation/output overhead; these are static counts, not measurements. Supplied discovery-0023 additionally gives 4096 contiguous one-million-character pieces sharing a 1 MB physical range (4096000000 logical characters within u32). Public read/conversion and native read_document refusal-hint parsing reach the same expansion before returning a format diagnostic; no runtime resource measurement is claimed. Additional supplied static framing: 4096 pieces reusing one 64 KiB compressed extent describe 268435456 logical characters; continuity and source-bound checks permit the reuse. This is not a measured allocation. Assigned discovery-0031 supplies 4000 one-million-character logical pieces sharing about 1 MB of physical text with about 48 KB of piece metadata, requesting about four billion StoryChars. CP continuity, checked offsets and physically bounded CFB traversal do not bound logical expansion; counts are static.\n Assigned discovery-0032 supplies 65535 contiguous pieces of 65535 compressed characters sharing one 65535-byte physical range: 4294836225 logical CPs within u32 and about 786 KB of piece records. These are static counts.\n\nSeverity: medium\n\nCategory: Memory allocation with excessive size value Unbounded decoded document expansion Unbounded logical text expansion Logical data amplification in legacy document decoding\n\nWeaknesses: CWE-789, CWE-770, CWE-400, CWE-409\n\nRemediation:\nIntroduce configurable legacy physical-input, piece-count, cumulative logical-character, retained-metadata and expansion-work budgets. Check FIB ccp_text and checked cumulative piece lengths before materialization, charge every decoded character including repeated physical aliases, and return typed refusals through read/doc_to_markdown/doc_to_docx and CLI callers. Preserve legitimate fast-save aliases and split UTF-16 surrogate handling within budget. Replace full legacy parsing used only for diagnostic refusal hints with a cheap bounded probe, or apply the same logical/work budgets before that parser runs.\nAdditional retained remediation detail: Introduce a configurable legacy logical-character/output budget, check ccp_text and cumulative decoded piece work before materialization, and enforce it during decoding. Preserve valid reuse while returning a typed refusal for excessive expansion.\n\nRemediation tests:\n- Aliased physical text pieces exceeding a small logical-character budget must refuse before StoryChar allocation.\n- Preserve legitimate shared/fast-save pieces within the configured budget; cover maximum ccp_text and cumulative metadata budgets.\n- Use small repeated physical aliases with a deliberately small decoded budget; assert typed refusal before expanding beyond it.\n- Retain valid aliases, split UTF-16 surrogate handling and continuity/error cases.\n- Use a small physical text stream with many increasing CP ranges sharing FC backing; assert a budget error before materialization.\n- Retain tests accepting legitimate short FC aliases and refusing decreasing CPs, gaps and out-of-range physical references.\n- A compact valid compound file with repeated FC pieces and excessive logical CP coverage must be refused before expansion.\n- Boundary-sized permitted stories and legitimate reused text pieces should import successfully.\n- CLI conversion must apply the same logical expansion budget as direct library calls.\n- A valid piece table with increasing CPs but many references to one short physical range must return a resource-limit error before allocating proportional to its claimed story.\n- Preserve legitimate repeated/fast-save pieces within the configured decoded-text limit.\n- Reject compact piece tables whose repeated physical spans exceed the logical-character/work budget.\n- Preserve legitimate fast-saved pieces and reject excessive logical stories before allocation.\n- Repeated physical ranges cannot exceed the logical-character/decoded-output budget.\n- Valid fast-save piece reuse remains supported within bounds.\n- Provide many increasing logical piece ranges sharing a physical range; require a logical-character budget refusal before StoryChar growth.\n- Assert adversarial small-input amplification is refused within active resource limits.\n- Cover the inclusive accepted limits and preserve legitimate document behavior.\n- Refuse repeated-span documents whose cumulative decoded story exceeds an explicit small test budget.\n- Validate the logical budget before allocating StoryChar state.\n- Keep valid physical span reuse supported below the budget.\n- Use many contiguous logical pieces reusing a small physical byte range and assert refusal before record expansion.\n- Cover checked cumulative length accounting, non-main-story ranges and both compressed and UTF-16 text.\n- Use a small logical-character budget with repeated physical ranges and verify typed refusal before expansion.\n- Cover exact budget boundary, legitimate reused text ranges, surrogate pairs and native CLI refusal-hint behavior.\n- Add deterministic bounded-input regression coverage around the broken control; for filesystem staging use isolated integration cases with pre-existing symlink entries and verify unrelated files remain unchanged.\n- Use a small deterministic document with extreme semantic values or alternate valid OPC paths and assert controlled refusal or complete selected sanitization before expensive processing.\n- Reject repeated-fc pieces above a small configured logical budget while preserving legitimate fast-saved DOC files.\n- Ensure CLI unsupported-input diagnostics do not fully materialize legacy text.\n- Reject a small DOC whose reused pieces exceed a small configured decoded-character budget.\n- Accept repeated physical pieces when their total logical expansion stays within the budget.\n- Construct a small repeated-range piece table and assert refusal before large allocation.\n- Accept legitimate reused ranges when decoded length remains below budget.\n- Contiguous CP pieces repeatedly aliasing one physical range must be refused at the logical expansion budget before large allocation.\n- Use deterministic small budgets with overlapping physical piece references and assert early refusal before logical expansion.\n- Test accepted fast-save/reused pieces below budget, CP continuity errors and physical offset bounds.\n\nPreventive controls:\n- Budget logical processing work and representation expansion, and bind preflight validation to the actual consumer's selected input.\n- Give legacy parsing the same explicit admission-budget contract as DOCX parsing, including logical output work."
          },
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "codexSecurity/v1": "codex-security/v1:sha256:e900bd901d8a5e0b6456c764267c37bafec16873a2e3e5cdef45c2d97f835dfb"
          },
