          ],
          "message": {
            "text": "Rendering an HTML chunk can allocate gigabytes from a single colspan value\n\nA document author can place a huge positive colspan in a small embedded HTML/MHT altChunk table. Ordinary DOCX rendering generates one grid XML element per effective column after original package admission and before DOM/layout checks. A count of 1000000000 drives a billion appends; 4294967295 generates over 85 GB of markup, exhausting CPU/memory. The supplied positive 4294967295 framing fits both 32-bit and 64-bit usize and needs no arithmetic overflow. Assigned discovery-0031 corroborates colspan=1000000000 causing tens of gigabytes of generated XML and work before layout; rejecting invalid, negative or zero spans does not bound valid positive values.\n Assigned discovery-0032 supplies a static colspan=1000000000 framing of one billion 20-byte grid elements, about 20 GB of generated text before DOM construction; this is not a runtime measurement.\n\nSeverity: medium\n\nCategory: Uncontrolled resource allocation Unbounded allocation from HTML numeric input Unbounded generated markup expansion Unbounded allocation through HTML table expansion\n\nWeaknesses: CWE-789, CWE-400, CWE-770, CWE-834\n\nRemediation:\nEnforce a practical maximum cell span and checked per-row/aggregate column counts before generating any table XML. Charge all HTML/MHT-generated WML, including repeated/nested tables, to a cumulative byte/work budget inside altChunk expansion and return a typed input-limit refusal before allocation or DOM construction.\nAdditional retained remediation detail: Bound individual spans and aggregate columns before summation or iteration, use checked arithmetic, and impose a cumulative generated-WML byte/element budget shared across every HTML/MHT altChunk. Refuse excessive expansion with a typed input-limit error before allocation.\n\nRemediation tests:\n- A tiny HTML chunk with colspan=1000000000 must refuse before allocation.\n- Check multi-cell aggregate span overflow and repeated/nested table expansion budgets.\n- Excessive individual/cumulative colspan in HTML and MHT must fail with bounded output/work.\n- Cover malformed spans, overflow and nested tables.\n- Preserve valid small HTML tables.\n- Unit tests with a small configured column cap cover the inclusive limit, one above it, oversized numeric values, and sums of several otherwise valid spans.\n- Integration test through a DOCX altChunk confirms refusal occurs before grid generation.\n- Use a tiny in-memory altChunk HTML with excessive td/th colspan and assert a prompt typed refusal without output growth.\n- Exercise cumulative spans across multiple cells, numeric overflow and valid inclusive limits.\n- A tiny altChunk with colspan 1000000000 must fail before large output allocation.\n- Cover inclusive limits, cumulative spans across cells, integer overflow, nested tables, and both HTML and MHT import.\n- Reject an oversized colspan in a small admitted DOCX without allocating proportional output.\n- Cover aggregate spans across multiple cells and arithmetic overflow while preserving normal merged tables.\n- A one-cell HTML table with an excessive numeric colspan returns a typed refusal without allocating proportional output.\n- Multiple spans cannot overflow the cumulative count or bypass the generated-grid limit.\n- Ordinary bounded colspan tables retain rendering behavior.\n- Reject huge representable positive colspan through public rendering while retaining ordinary span behavior.\n- Verify many bounded spans cannot exceed the cumulative table/output budget.\n- Verify row-span sum overflow returns a refusal on native and wasm32.\n- Exercise a compact HTML chunk with huge positive colspan and require bounded refusal before WML generation.\n- Refuse a single td/th with colspan=4294967295 without allocating proportional output.\n- Refuse rows whose individually valid spans exceed the aggregate column budget; preserve ordinary merged-cell rendering.\n- Assert that a tiny HTML chunk with a huge colspan is refused before grid allocation.\n- Cover aggregate span overflow, multiple cells/chunks, and valid spans at the inclusive boundary.\n- Excessive colspan refuses before grid generation.\n- Ordinary spans render; aggregate spans and overflow refuse safely.\n- Refuse large HTML and MHT colspan before emitting column XML.\n- Verify ordinary table conversion succeeds within both column and generated-output budgets.\n- Reject a very large positive colspan before emitting grid columns.\n- Reject cumulative row-span overflow and excessive summed columns.\n- Verify normal small spans and zero/invalid-span fallback remain correct.\n- Verify rendering a DOCX carrying the oversized altChunk returns a bounded error.\n- Render an admitted DOCX with a huge HTML colspan and assert bounded typed refusal.\n- Check overflow in sums of several cell spans and cumulative expansion across tables.\n- Assert oversized colspan and excessive aggregate table columns return a typed error before generating the grid.\n- Test repeated references to the same chunk against an aggregate generated-output budget.\n- Oversized and cumulatively oversized HTML colspans must be refused before grid XML generation.\n- Refuse an admitted HTML altChunk with a huge positive colspan before generating grid XML.\n- Cover summed-span overflow and repeated/nested tables under a shared output budget.\n- Render a minimal admitted DOCX with an HTML altChunk and a very large span; assert a bounded typed refusal without large allocation.\n- Check aggregate spans across multiple cells, nested tables, multiple chunks, and arithmetic-overflow values; retain ordinary small colspan rendering.\n\nPreventive controls:\n- Charge representation expansion to explicit output/work budgets rather than relying only on compressed/inflated input limits.\n- Budget derived representations before materialization, including post-admission HTML/MHT expansion.\n- Apply resource limits to decoded/expanded representations, not only archive bytes.\n- Apply resource budgets at representation changes, including generated WML."
          },
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "codexSecurity/v1": "codex-security/v1:sha256:4ba6e152fed26e7c99ea06ef90cd76682fce35b891a82bf755c9149543ed3537"
          },
