            {
              "message": {
                "text": "evidence:dedup0028-n4-field-public"
              },
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/legacy_doc.rs"
                },
                "region": {
                  "endLine": 237,
                  "startLine": 225
                }
              }
            }
          ],
          "message": {
            "text": "Reading or converting nested legacy DOC fields can monopolize the processing thread\n\nAn attacker-authored Word 97\u20132003 DOC can repeatedly begin fields and enter their result sections, leaving an increasingly long all-false field stack. Story::blocks scans the entire stack before every input character, giving quadratic CPU work during reading/conversion and even the native Word-input refusal hint. Assigned discovery-0031 supplies a balanced result-state framing with approximately 100000 nested fields in a few hundred kilobytes, implying billions of boolean inspections. This is heap-stack scanning CPU cost, not recursive call-stack overflow.\n\nSeverity: medium\n\nCategory: Algorithmic complexity in nested field processing\n\nWeaknesses: CWE-407, CWE-400\n\nRemediation:\nMaintain an O(1) active-code-frame count, updating it correctly on begin, separator and end while preserving suppression under any code ancestor. Enforce explicit field-nesting and processing-work budgets for balanced and malformed legacy field streams before excessive work. Keep legacy diagnostic probes cheap and bounded, or enforce the same field/work budgets before invoking the full reader solely for a refusal hint.\n\nRemediation tests:\n- Verify linear field-processing work for deeply nested begin/separator/result/end sequences.\n- Cover mixed code/result nesting to retain the rule that any code ancestor suppresses text.\n- Nested result fields must consume linear work with a maintained active-code count.\n- Over-depth balanced and unbalanced fields must refuse within an explicit depth budget.\n- Assert work scales linearly for deeply nested balanced result-phase fields.\n- Preserve nested-code suppression semantics, repeated separators and unmatched field-marker behavior.\n- Extend existing nested-field fixtures with deeply nested balanced result fields.\n- Assert linear parser operations with a deterministic counter rather than wall-clock timing.\n- Check correctness and configured depth refusal.\n- Use deterministic work counters to show linear processing of deeply nested field-result sequences.\n- Refuse nesting above the configured bound and preserve ordinary nested-field semantics.\n- Use a deterministic parser test with many nested result-phase fields; assert bounded work using an instrumented operation count and correct ordinary field output.\n- Bound work for deeply nested result fields and enforce a maximum depth.\n- Preserve nested code/result visibility semantics without rescanning the complete stack per character.\n- Deep nested result fields process in linear time or return a typed depth refusal.\n- Nested code/result semantics retain expected text inclusion.\n- Use a deterministic parser work counter on nested field-start/separator/result sequences and assert linear growth or an explicit depth refusal.\n- Assert adversarial small-input amplification is refused within active resource limits.\n- Cover the inclusive accepted limits and preserve legitimate document behavior.\n- Use deterministic operation counters to assert linear work for nested result fields.\n- Test a field-depth limit and exact accepted boundary.\n- Preserve existing nested-field visible-text behavior.\n- Validate nested result/code transitions under a small depth budget and typed over-limit refusal.\n- Use an operation-count or pure-state test to establish linear processing rather than a fragile wall-clock threshold.\n- Add deterministic bounded-input regression coverage around the broken control; for filesystem staging use isolated integration cases with pre-existing symlink entries and verify unrelated files remain unchanged.\n- Use a small deterministic document with extreme semantic values or alternate valid OPC paths and assert controlled refusal or complete selected sanitization before expensive processing.\n- Verify deeply nested result-state fields are linear-time or refused at the configured depth.\n- Preserve nested-field code/result semantics when transitioning and popping.\n- Use a deterministic operation counter to verify linear work for many balanced nested result fields.\n- Verify mixed parent-code and child-result behavior remains correct across separators and ends.\n- Assert bounded work for increasing counts of nested separated fields and balanced endings.\n- Preserve existing nested-field display semantics while updating the active-field counter.\n- Use a deterministic work counter on separated nested fields and assert linear processing as depth increases.\n- Test nested code/result transitions and an explicit excessive-depth refusal without wall-clock assertions.\n- Balanced deeply nested field results must have linear state-inspection cost or refuse at a documented nesting budget.\n\nPreventive controls:\n- Budget logical processing work and representation expansion, and bind preflight validation to the actual consumer's selected input."
          },
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "codexSecurity/v1": "codex-security/v1:sha256:e04b07100c3dbba403d673adacc41652b9fc14ee13bf26abea96b319ccc09224"
          },
          "properties": {
            "category": "Algorithmic complexity in nested field processing",
            "confidence": "high",
