          "message": {
            "text": "Tolerated XML syntax can hide recursive elements from admission\n\nA DOCX author can place nested element text inside an XML comment after an unquoted attribute. Admission counts only shallow quick-xml events, while the permissive DOM parser consumes that attribute differently and recursively interprets the hidden tags. The normal .xml path can therefore exhaust the host stack despite max_xml_depth.\n\nSeverity: medium\n\nCategory: Parser interpretation conflict XML depth bypass\n\nWeaknesses: CWE-436, CWE-674\n\nRemediation:\nMake admission and DOM construction consume the same grammar, reject unsupported DTDs at package admission, parse unquoted values correctly or refuse them, and enforce a hard depth budget inside DOM construction.\n\nRemediation tests:\n- Package processing must safely refuse or correctly parse an unquoted attribute followed by a comment containing deeply nested tag text.\n- A DTD containing markup inside a quoted entity literal must be refused before raw DOM parsing; no external fetch should occur.\n- Run the same fixtures through admission and the actual consumer and assert matching structure/depth semantics."
          },
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "codexSecurity/v1": "codex-security/v1:sha256:28ac0dc9f06432d472b7d3f883fb88cb350a20747a1ffa29fa7cb8cd84711d8c"
          },
          "properties": {
            "category": "Parser interpretation conflict / XML depth bypass",
            "confidence": "high",
            "findingId": "csf_c1fd47c87c992981879d9be6",
            "occurrenceId": "occ_978df50bc58d58b1e128e552",
            "severity": "medium"
          },
          "ruleId": "xml-policy.admission-dom-parser-differential",
          "ruleIndex": 84
        },
