          "message": {
            "text": "Unbounded list levels cause billions of iterations or allocations\n\nParagraph and numbering-definition ilvl attributes accept arbitrary u32 values. Resolving an undefined high level scans every lower integer; defining the high level instead drives counter initialization and label substitution loops over the same enormous range. Abstract definitions and full lvlOverride records accept the same unchecked level IDs; both missing-level reverse search and defined-level counter/label work require bounded handling.\n Assigned discovery-0032 preserves the valid numbering-instance prerequisite and the distinct billion-level defined-counter variant as well as undefined-level backwards search.\n\nSeverity: medium\n\nCategory: Uncontrolled resource consumption Unchecked input controlling loop bounds\n\nWeaknesses: CWE-834, CWE-606\n\nRemediation:\nValidate the supported ilvl domain in paragraph, style, abstract and override sources before use. Find fallback levels among actual defined keys and initialize/substitute only supported or defined levels; avoid any iteration or allocation proportional to an untrusted numeric level. Return a typed refusal for unsupported level metadata rather than attempting dense counter or placeholder materialization.\nAdditional retained remediation detail: Validate paragraph and numbering-definition level IDs against the supported 0..=8 range. Resolve ancestors by actual defined keys and bound synthesized counters and marker iteration independently of untrusted integer values.\n\nRemediation tests:\n- Maximum u32 ilvl with a level-zero list must refuse or resolve in bounded work.\n- A high defined level and override must not allocate lower-level counters; retain valid nine-level numbering.\n- Exercise huge paragraph ilvl against level-0 numbering and assert fast typed refusal.\n- Exercise huge definition/override levels and assert no expanded counter allocation; retain ordinary 0..8 behavior.\n- Reject excessive paragraph, style, abstract and override level indexes before iteration.\n- Verify both undefined-high and defined-high level inputs fail promptly while ordinary inherited numbering remains correct.\n- A huge undefined ilvl returns promptly with a typed refusal or the bounded fallback.\n- Defined and ordinary missing levels retain expected Word-compatible fallback.\n- Reject or safely normalize u32-max paragraph, abstract and override levels.\n- Verify sparse definitions do not trigger iteration proportional to an undefined numeric index.\n- Retain ordinary multilevel numbering behavior.\n- Public rendering tests for maximum, over-limit and u32::MAX paragraph/abstract numbering levels should refuse promptly with bounded memory.\n- Cover fallback-to-parent numbering and sparse definitions without dense iteration.\n- Deterministic boundary tests for huge paragraph/abstract/override levels should assert prompt refusal without attempting large loops; preserve ordinary parent-level fallback and counter semantics.\n- Use a small deterministic document with extreme semantic values or alternate valid OPC paths and assert controlled refusal or complete selected sanitization before expensive processing.\n- Render large defined and undefined ilvl values and assert a bounded typed refusal.\n- Test valid 0..=8 levels, valid parent inheritance, style-derived numbering and revised-list paths.\n\nPreventive controls:\n- Use a validated bounded numbering-level type throughout the renderer."
          },
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "codexSecurity/v1": "codex-security/v1:sha256:85b99e4c3145aecaeca3926d1b91153a8829a26d51d928e12332034d7672ffe5"
