          "message": {
            "text": "Python CLI output/plan and npm direct-output symlinks can overwrite unrelated files\n\nPython and npm ordinary-output helpers check pathname existence separately from truncating writes; a local writer of the selected directory can replace an output name and redirect generated bytes to a victim-writable file. Python plan recording additionally uses a predictable basename/PID partial pathname without exclusive creation. The assigned report also corroborates npm plan staging, which is represented by the separately retained symlink-following.plan-staging finding. Without --force, a stable symlink to an existing referent is refused; post-preflight substitution bypasses that refusal, and a dangling link can pass the existence test. Filesystem/OS protections and shared-directory authority remain prerequisites. The shared writers also publish read/compare/revision results, document/PDF/PNG and font/render products, edit/visual-diff bundles, field-update reports, and repair outputs. Assigned Python and npm diff/bundle traces explicitly include dangling destination links permitting unintended file creation without --force, and forced existing-member links redirecting bundle/report writes; stable existing referents are refused in the ordinary nonracing no-force case.\n Assigned discovery-0032 corroborates Python PDF/PNG publication and Node renderTo, with conversion between existence preflight and final write; shared mutable directory entries and distinct attacker/victim authority remain essential.\n\nSeverity: low\n\nCategory: Unsafe filesystem publication symbolic link following Time of check time of use output race\n\nWeaknesses: CWE-61, CWE-367\n\nRemediation:\nPublish Python/Node ordinary outputs, bundles and reports through held-directory authority and exclusive private staging. Atomically install without replacement when --force is false, or atomically replace the authorized destination entry when true; never truncate through a mutable final pathname. Apply exclusive unpredictable regular-file staging beside the authorized target to Python plan recording. Node recordPlan staging retains its separately established repair and identity. Expose/reuse native publication for Python CLI outputs with overwrite authorization passed into the commit operation; Node host publication must provide equivalent exclusive staging and atomic no-clobber/replacement. Preserve the documented final-output rule: forced publication replaces the selected symlink directory entry itself and leaves its referent unchanged; no-force installation must refuse an already occupied destination, including a dangling symlink.\nAdditional retained remediation detail: Use staged verified publication with held parent-directory authority for both wrappers. Carry overwrite authorization through commit and atomically refuse any existing destination when force is false; do not rely on a preliminary exists check.\n\nRemediation tests:\n- Integration: preplant a symlink at the plan partial path and assert its referent remains unchanged.\n- Integration: replace an output between preflight and publication and assert no unrelated referent is modified.\n- Verify atomic no-clobber and existing/dangling symlink handling for ordinary outputs, reports and edit bundles in both CLI adapters.\n- Integration-test destination substitution between preflight and commit for both adapters and verify outside referents remain unchanged.\n- Test dangling symlinks, newly occupied outputs, and authorized --force replacement.\n- Insert a destination between preflight and publication and verify no-force refuses it without changing its referent.\n- Cover all side products and verify --force replaces only the selected directory entry rather than following a substituted link.\n- Insert an output symlink between preflight and commit; verify no-force refuses it and preserves the referent.\n- Verify forced publication replaces the entry without modifying its symlink referent.\n- Apply checks to PDF/PNG, edited outputs, read exports, comparison and report publication.\n- Filesystem integration test precreates staging symlink to a sentinel file and verifies refusal without changing sentinel.\n- Exercise concurrent destination creation with overwrite false and ensure atomic failure; verify forced output replaces link entry without following it.\n- Integration test a destination link with and without overwrite authorization; only the directory entry may be replaced.\n- Race a destination creation between preparation and commit; no-clobber must fail atomically.\n- Test forced bundle members that are symlinks.\n- Insert a symlink between precheck and publication and assert no target data changes without --force.\n- Exercise each adapter's output and side-report routes through the shared safe publication mechanism.\n- No-force output refuses a preexisting dangling symlink and leaves its referent absent.\n- Forced output replaces the selected symlink itself and preserves its referent.\n- A destination created or replaced after the initial check is never truncated without overwrite authority.\n- Exercise equivalent document, view, report, render and bundle outputs in both wrappers.\n- With a deterministic fake publication backend, insert a destination after preflight and assert no-force commit refuses without touching its referent.\n- Use filesystem integration tests for symlink replacement and preserve native/MCP no-clobber behavior.\n\nPreventive controls:\n- Enforce overwrite policy at the commit operation, never only at a prior existence check."
          },
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "codexSecurity/v1": "codex-security/v1:sha256:4a636bd23017f65bc97224f8c1e1b01605b276b8780cc5a374f26c3f35a69d33"
          },
          "properties": {
            "category": "Unsafe filesystem publication / symbolic link following\nTime-of-check/time-of-use output race",
            "confidence": "high",
            "findingId": "csf_b45e3ea11925b4220c435786",
            "occurrenceId": "occ_a7e0f3394191f153db1ed22f",
            "severity": "low"
          },
          "ruleId": "unsafe-filesystem-publication.symlink-following",
          "ruleIndex": 77
        },
