          ],
          "message": {
            "text": "Rendering Chicago numbering can allocate gigabytes for one short counter\n\nDOCX list starts and section page starts accept u32 values, while Chicago numbering repeats a symbol about one time per four counter values. A short attacker-controlled start value can therefore allocate a multi-gigabyte label during normal rendering.\n\nSeverity: medium\n\nCategory: Unbounded generated string allocation\n\nWeaknesses: CWE-789\n\nRemediation:\nEnforce a maximum generated label length before symbol repetition and return a typed resource refusal or bounded documented fallback. Reuse the guard for every Chicago formatter consumer, including list, page and note numbering.\n\nRemediation tests:\n- Exercise maximum u32 Chicago list start and startOverride without large allocation.\n- Test Chicago section page starts and note-label consumers.\n- Preserve ordinary multi-symbol Chicago sequences."
          },
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "codexSecurity/v1": "codex-security/v1:sha256:ca047984cf86753d84b615483b6f33b16f531125beae9d480b5be7ac7688a98f"
          },
          "properties": {
            "category": "Unbounded generated string allocation",
            "confidence": "high",
            "findingId": "csf_3339fb9302ddc111d50992db",
            "occurrenceId": "occ_ba36936a54c597da41611d03",
            "severity": "medium"
          },
          "ruleId": "resource-exhaustion.chicago-number-label",
          "ruleIndex": 42
        },
