          ],
          "message": {
            "text": "Pull-request code runs on shared self-hosted infrastructure\n\nA lower-trust PR admitted to run can change a local action, build script, test or generator and execute it with self-hosted runner privileges. Four workflows share this missing isolation boundary; the local cache action documents private tokenless cache connectivity and reused checkout/disk. A permitted run also inherits the documented private Docker network and tokenless mbx-cache endpoint; cache poisoning, persistence and additional internal service access remain unverified deployment-dependent outcomes. Assigned discovery-0031 corroborates permitted fork execution across all four workflows and the documented tokenless private Docker cache network/reused checkouts. contents:read, persist-credentials:false and normal client-side PR cache-read policy do not establish process/network isolation. Actual approvals, worker lifecycle and server authorization remain unverified.\n\nSeverity: medium\n\nCategory: CI isolation of untrusted code\n\nWeaknesses: CWE-829\n\nRemediation:\nRun lower-trust PR builds/tests/generators on GitHub-hosted or one-job disposable runners without trusted writable state or private infrastructure connectivity. Separate trusted main runners and enforce cache write authorization server-side. Apply consistently to ci, adoption, docs and md-roundtrip.\n\nRemediation tests:\n- Verify every PR-reachable job selects isolated runner infrastructure.\n- Verify PR runners cannot access trusted cache writes or retained trusted-job state.\n- Check that fork/proposal events cannot select the shared self-hosted pool.\n- Validate trusted branch jobs preserve intended build/cache behavior.\n- Assert every pull_request job is routed to an isolated runner pool or rejects untrusted head repositories.\n- Verify an untrusted build cannot reach the internal cache or read state from an earlier trusted job.\n- Statically assert all PR-executing jobs use isolated ephemeral runner routing and cannot target trusted runner groups.\n- Verify server-side cache authorization and teardown in infrastructure integration checks without granting contributor code persistent credentials.\n- Verify a fork PR cannot schedule work on shared trusted runners or access their private cache/network.\n- Review effective Actions approval policy, runner lifecycle and server-side cache authorization before asserting isolation.\n\nPreventive controls:\n- Separate proposed-code execution from persistent/private build infrastructure.\n- Separate untrusted PR compute and trusted push/release infrastructure."
          },
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "codexSecurity/v1": "codex-security/v1:sha256:9cfc568d7ca52259f007434f109130bacb1056d94fdcf8e22efccdcf5159de43"
