          "message": {
            "text": "Entity decoding repeatedly scans the input suffix\n\nHTML altChunk text with bare ampersands and the public XML entity decoder repeatedly search the remaining suffix for ';', yielding quadratic CPU work. A valid admitted .rels Target containing repeated &amp; is normalized upstream and redundantly decoded by PartFs, reaching the same XML sink during rendering, inspection, revision processing or comparison even when the target part does not exist. An additional admitted-main-XML route uses an unquoted value whose first character never reappears: the DOM consumes through EOF and decodes a bare-ampersand comment as an attribute, reaching the same quadratic XML decoder. Ordinary bare-ampersand text is rejected upstream and is not this proof. The same HTML decoder is also reached by Markdown-to-DOCX raw HTML blocks and attribute values; its ten-byte accepted-entity bound is applied after the unbounded search. Assigned discovery-0031 corroborates both established decoders independently: ordinary HTML/MHT paragraph text reaches the HTML helper even when script/style content is skipped, while an internal fontTable target reaches redundant XML decoding before any absent-part lookup. Its supplied offline dependency evidence identifies oxml-opc 0.14.2 and quick-xml 0.41.0 attribute normalization, independently supported by the repository target round-trip test. No reducer dependency inspection occurred.\n\nSeverity: medium\n\nCategory: Algorithmic complexity denial of service\n\nWeaknesses: CWE-407\n\nRemediation:\nScan entities once without revisiting suffixes. In HTML, bound the search to the supported entity-length window before looking for ';'; give XML decoding a monotonic parser or bounded entity grammar. Remove PartFs's redundant relationship decoding when upstream supplies normalized attributes; retain the linear XML-helper repair for its other callers and the separate bounded HTML-helper repair. Preserve canonical literal '&amp;' part names and one-time entity normalization through round trips. Preserve the standalone XML helper's documented permissive output for unsupported or unterminated references while making its work linear. Apply practical relationship-target length/work bounds as defense in depth, preserving exactly one normalization pass and the standalone helper's linear permissive behavior. Align admission/DOM attribute token boundaries or reject unsupported unquoted syntax as defense in depth. Preserve the linear XML decoder fix for this comment-to-attribute route; removing only the redundant relationship decode does not cover it. Apply the bounded HTML helper consistently to Markdown raw-HTML block text and attribute consumers, using UTF-8-safe bounded lookahead and preserving recognized entities, malformed literal retention and multibyte text.\n\nRemediation tests:\n- Deterministic operation-count tests for long bare-ampersand runs must scale linearly.\n- Preserve predefined/numeric entity decoding and literal retention of unknown/truncated entities in both helpers.\n- Add deterministic regression inputs for the source-supported differential and assert a typed bounded refusal.\n- Verify ordinary compatible documents still process under unchanged limits.\n- Use valid .rels Targets with repeated &amp; through revision processing and comparison, including nonexistent internal targets; verify bounded linear work and one-time entity normalization. Keep malformed-reference literal preservation and ordinary valid target behavior.\n- Resolve targets containing many encoded literal ampersands with linear work.\n- Preserve literal '&amp;' part names without repeated decoding and round-trip corruption.\n- Verify an escaped-ampersand relationship resolves with exactly one decode.\n- Use instrumented scan-step assertions for long ampersand-only strings and unknown entities to ensure linear work.\n- Keep legitimate encoded targets resolving correctly.\n- Resolve a legal relationship containing many escaped ampersands with linear work and correct literal target preservation.\n- Retain handling of numeric/predefined entities and nonexistent internal targets without double-decoding them.\n- Many raw ampersands require linear scan work.\n- Named/numeric entity behavior and malformed suffix handling remain correct.\n- Verify repeated malformed ampersands require linear work through the public HTML import path.\n- Retain unknown/numeric/predefined entity behavior.\n- Count bounded lookahead steps for ampersand-heavy semicolon-free text and assert work proportional to input length.\n- Assert relationship target decoding occurs once; use deterministic counted work for a long escaped-ampersand target and malformed public decoder strings.\n- Regression-test literal ampersands in parsed targets for exactly-once decoding.\n- Count scanner steps for long ampersand targets and assert linear growth without wall-clock unit assertions.\n- Integration-test rendering a document whose missing internal story target contains a long encoded-ampersand sequence.\n- Inspect and compare valid relationships with long escaped-ampersand targets and verify bounded linear work/refusal.\n- Preserve correct resolution of names with a literal ampersand or semicolon after exactly one XML decode.\n- Deterministic boundary regression tests should assert typed refusal or bounded work for the identified tokenizer/classification/count discrepancy, with normal supported inputs preserved.\n- Malformed ampersand runs require linear bounded search work.\n- Recognized entities and multibyte text preserve current output.\n- Both altChunk and Markdown HTML callers use the bounded helper.\n- Inspect a valid &amp;-heavy target with work proportional to attribute length.\n- Ensure literal encoded entity-looking filenames retain exactly one decoding step.\n- Use a deterministic comparison/work counter to establish linear bounded scanning for long runs of unterminated ampersands.\n- A relationship target containing many encoded ampersands must resolve or fail in linear work without a second entity decode.\n\nPreventive controls:\n- Specify encoded-versus-decoded contracts at parser adapters and avoid repeated representation decoding."
          },
          "partialFingerprints": {
