          "message": {
            "text": "Nested semantic fields multiply retained instructions and hash work\n\nField salting copies each instruction into every open semantic field and materializes the full chain again for every atom. Sibling fldChar begin/end markers can create arbitrarily deep field nesting at shallow XML depth, amplifying a modest instruction into gigabytes.\n\nSeverity: medium\n\nCategory: Unbounded allocation and work from semantic field nesting\n\nWeaknesses: CWE-770\n\nRemediation:\nBound semantic field depth and total instruction/hash work before copying. Use shared instruction representations and incremental/compositional field hashes instead of materializing every full nested code chain; return a typed refusal when budgets are exceeded.\n\nRemediation tests:\n- Compare genuinely different documents with shallow XML and sibling nested field begin/separate/end markers under a small semantic-field/instruction/work budget; require typed refusal before instruction or salted-chain amplification.\n- Preserve normal field semantics, nested code/result relationships, hash discrimination and balanced/malformed field-marker handling without materializing full instruction chains per atom."
          },
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "codexSecurity/v1": "codex-security/v1:sha256:4d63b1326522e08a2d7226b44001aa884e756158966c93279183ef33bdcebe96"
          },
          "properties": {
            "category": "Unbounded allocation and work from semantic field nesting",
            "confidence": "high",
            "findingId": "csf_30d7378aa76256da61dfe8fc",
            "occurrenceId": "occ_c128ca604b70bd2303ce3482",
            "severity": "medium"
          },
          "ruleId": "nested-field-instruction-amplification",
          "ruleIndex": 29
        },
        {
