          "message": {
            "text": "ZIP footer and count interpretation mismatch bypasses entry-count admission\n\nThe independent preindex footer parser can count a different EOCD or count field from zip 9.0.5. False small-count EOCDs in valid comments can be skipped by zip; a same-footer total=3/on-this-disk=10001 also passes max_entries=10000 while zip indexes 10001. ZIP64 size/offset triggers differ too. Neither admission nor PartFs enforces the actual opened count; duplicate detection also trusts the unrelated declared count. Another supplied false-footer proof uses an EOCD-shaped record inside the real archive comment whose own comment length is impossible; preindex trusts its low count while the ZIP reader rejects it and backtracks.\n\nSeverity: medium\n\nCategory: Input validation ZIP parser differential ZIP metadata resource policy bypass\n\nWeaknesses: CWE-20, CWE-400, CWE-770\n\nRemediation:\nUse the delegated reader's authoritative validated EOCD/ZIP64 selection, consistent ZIP32 total/per-disk fields and all ZIP64 total/size/offset triggers. Bound raw central-record traversal/count before index allocation, reject inconsistent unsupported metadata, and retain a post-open active archive.len cap as defense in depth; that later check cannot undo earlier allocation. Reconcile selected raw counts with indexed counts for exact duplicates, including name collapse, comments, ZIP64 and prepended data. Detect raw exact-name duplicates directly or through the reader's duplicate flag rather than relying solely on a count-shortfall inference.\n\nRemediation tests:\n- A false zero-count EOCD with inconsistent disk fields in a real comment must not bypass max_entries.\n- Exact duplicate central records must still be detected when footer-like signatures occur in comments, including ZIP64 cases.\n- A valid over-count ZIP with invalid EOCD-like bytes in its comment must fail the entry budget before indexing growth.\n- Cover normal comments containing signature bytes, ZIP64 and prepended data.\n- Keep duplicate-part and exact-entry-bound behavior.\n- Test inconsistent ZIP32 total/per-disk counts at small max_entries limits.\n- Test ZIP64 activated by size/offset sentinels, false footer signatures in comments and duplicate-count cases.\n- Add deterministic regression inputs for the source-supported differential and assert a typed bounded refusal.\n- Verify ordinary compatible documents still process under unchanged limits.\n- Conflicting +8/+10 ZIP32 counts must be refused before archive indexing.\n- A max_entries boundary package succeeds and one over the boundary fails for admission and direct PartFs.\n- Validate EOCD selection consistently when comments contain footer signatures.\n- Refuse nonempty ZIP32 archives with total/on-disk count disagreement before excessive indexing.\n- Verify both admission and PartFs enforce tighter caller max_entries against the actual archive.\n- Reject over-budget archives with a false zero-count EOCD in the real comment.\n- Cover ordinary/ZIP64 candidate fallback and true duplicate detection.\n- Verify direct PartFs and public admission enforce the same active count budget.\n- Reject disagreeing +8/+10 counts with excess unique entries and exact duplicate names.\n- Cover ZIP64 footer selection and inclusive valid entry limits.\n- Use harmless small entry limits with a later invalid EOCD candidate and earlier valid archive; assert entry-budget refusal rather than acceptance.\n- Cover ZIP64, comments containing EOCD signatures, duplicate-entry checks and self-extracting stubs with the same validated record selection.\n- Deterministic boundary regression tests should assert typed refusal or bounded work for the identified tokenizer/classification/count discrepancy, with normal supported inputs preserved.\n- Use a small deterministic document with extreme semantic values or alternate valid OPC paths and assert controlled refusal or complete selected sanitization before expensive processing.\n- An archive with total=1 and on-disk count above max_entries refuses before indexing.\n- Post-open count enforcement protects both admission and PartFs.\n- Normal agreeing counts and ZIP64 limits retain expected behavior.\n\nPreventive controls:\n- Budget logical processing work and representation expansion, and bind preflight validation to the actual consumer's selected input.\n- Use a single validated archive interpretation for pre-admission and consumption."
          },
          "partialFingerprints": {
