          "message": {
            "text": "Repeated relationship IDs trigger quadratic repair work\n\nOpening a package repairs identical duplicate relationship IDs. For n distinct IDs each repeated identically, each repair linearly searches an increasingly long Vec, producing quadratic CPU work on shallow metadata that fits existing byte and attribute limits. Assigned discovery-0031 supplies approximately 200000 shallow Relationship records with 100000 distinct identical duplicate pairs within a 64 MiB part, implying about five billion membership comparisons. Conflicting duplicates remain promptly refused; counts are static.\n\nSeverity: medium\n\nCategory: Algorithmic complexity denial of service\n\nWeaknesses: CWE-407\n\nRemediation:\nTrack already-reported duplicate IDs in a HashSet or alongside seen-map state, retaining a Vec only for deterministic reporting order. Optionally cap relationship-record/work totals before repair; test many distinct identical duplicate pairs while preserving deterministic repaired-ID order and conflicting-duplicate refusals. Apply an active relationship-record/work budget at metadata admission in addition to making duplicate membership near-linear.\n\nRemediation tests:\n- Use a deterministic operation-count/scaling test with many distinct identical duplicate pairs.\n- Retain behavior tests for conflicting duplicates and deterministic repaired-ID order at both package and part level.\n- Use operation counters to verify linear repair for many IDs each repeated twice.\n- Preserve identical-duplicate reporting order and refusal of conflicting duplicates.\n- Many distinct IDs each repeated twice must preserve first records and report each repair once with work scaling linearly.\n- Conflicting duplicates must still be refused.\n- Exercise many distinct identical duplicates and assert membership work stays linear.\n- Retain conflicting-duplicate refusal and deterministic repair order.\n- Use counted membership/work operations for N unique relationships followed by N identical copies; require linear repair and stable deterministic repaired-ID ordering.\n- Assert duplicate repair order and conflict rejection using many distinct identical duplicate pairs.\n- Use operation-count assertions or scaling checks on pure in-memory data to verify near-linear membership work.\n- Use a small deterministic document with extreme semantic values or alternate valid OPC paths and assert controlled refusal or complete selected sanitization before expensive processing.\n- Many distinct identical duplicated IDs must retain stable repair reporting with linear membership work."
          },
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "codexSecurity/v1": "codex-security/v1:sha256:1698a45cd66b2f998198b6a9226cd3afcd374653a0eb7622c1ce73cf3e9ff18d"
          },
          "properties": {
