          "message": {
            "text": "Flat LaTeX script chains bypass nesting limits and exhaust the writer stack\n\nConverting untrusted Markdown with a long flat superscript/subscript chain builds an attacker-deep AST despite the parser's nesting limit. The recursive OMML writer then exhausts the native stack or traps a WASM instance.\n\nSeverity: medium\n\nCategory: Uncontrolled recursion\n\nWeaknesses: CWE-674\n\nRemediation:\nBound actual AST depth and work when wrapping previous script/prime bases, and use iterative writing and safe nonrecursive destruction so rejection cannot itself drop an attacker-deep tree recursively.\n\nRemediation tests:\n- A flat long a^1^1 chain must refuse or flatten within a fixed AST-depth budget.\n- Exercise subscript/superscript alternation and prime wrapping, including writer and destruction after refusal.\n\nPreventive controls:\n- Bound the actual representation depth and processing work at the consuming boundary."
          },
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "codexSecurity/v1": "codex-security/v1:sha256:3e4cc84778e64f0c9d5c7d1593143441492faaf5d52163672c11e19d31a75a41"
