          "message": {
            "text": "Tiny Markdown table metadata can trigger unbounded cell allocation\n\nA caller-controlled cells paragraph range is converted into an unbounded count and used to append padding cells. A tiny Markdown table with a range such as p0-p1000000000 causes roughly a billion padding entries to be allocated before DOCX assembly, exhausting CPU and memory in Rust, Python, or WASM conversion hosts. The new corroboration explicitly matches two metadata ranges to a header and data row without breaks; MOST_GRID=64 and arithmetic overflow checks do not bound these valid row counts.\n\nSeverity: medium\n\nCategory: Uncontrolled resource consumption\n\nWeaknesses: CWE-789\n\nRemediation:\nValidate row_paragraphs with the same bounded range helper used elsewhere, impose a total table/output cell budget before allocation, and return a structured refusal rather than silently processing an oversized range.\n\nRemediation tests:\n- With a tiny one-row table, a range exceeding the configured table budget must fail promptly without allocating proportional output.\n- Cover valid ranges at the inclusive limit, descending/overflow ranges, multiple rows, and ranges with and without break markers across the shared public API.\n- Convert a two-row table with a huge first-row paragraph range and assert immediate typed refusal.\n- Verify ordinary table metadata round-trips and aggregate range budgets cannot overflow."
          },
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "codexSecurity/v1": "codex-security/v1:sha256:ff2874c02482f1933185b8c4d0ec9ec7fc4fb1770d25290335a4fa4a84ea5771"
          },
          "properties": {
            "category": "Uncontrolled resource consumption",
            "confidence": "high",
            "findingId": "csf_5ad7cba70ec2176a33e66f2a",
            "occurrenceId": "occ_e8ad0273230d455ab109d042",
            "severity": "medium"
          },
          "ruleId": "resource-exhaustion.markdown-table-range",
          "ruleIndex": 48
        },
