          "message": {
            "text": "Python and Node plan recording can overwrite unrelated files through staging symlinks\n\nThe Python and Node CLIs write plans through a predictable .<plan>.<PID>.partial pathname using nonexclusive truncating writes. A local actor who can create entries in the plan directory can preplace a symlink there and make the victim overwrite another file it can write, before the intended plan replacement.\n\nSeverity: low\n\nCategory: Improper link resolution in temporary file publication\n\nWeaknesses: CWE-59, CWE-377, CWE-61\n\nRemediation:\nUse exclusive, unpredictable staging owned by a private directory, hold the opened handle through writing, and rename that verified file. Reuse the native publication design or equivalent mkstemp/exclusive wx APIs and never follow existing staging entries. Python can reuse native publication; Node needs equivalent secure publication semantics.\n\nRemediation tests:\n- Preplace staging symlinks and assert neither adapter writes the referent or modifies the existing plan.\n- Verify concurrent recording cannot reuse/replace another writer's staging file.\n- Check cleanup only removes the recorder's own staging object.\n- Integration-test attacker-planted PID partial symlinks for both adapters; assert referent bytes remain unchanged and the operation refuses or safely stages elsewhere.\n- Test successful normal recording, dry-run and preview refusal.\n- In a filesystem integration test, preplant the old predictable .partial name as a symlink and verify recording leaves its referent unchanged.\n- Verify existing plans survive staging failure and normal plan-symlink behavior remains an explicit supported policy.\n- Precreate the old predictable partial pathname as a symlink and verify its referent remains unchanged in Python and Node.\n- Verify failed staging preserves the existing plan and cleans only operation-owned temporary entries.\n- Precreate the predicted partial as a symlink and verify both adapters preserve the referent.\n- Verify intended plan symlink semantics and cleanup remain correct.\n- Filesystem integration test precreates staging symlink to a sentinel file and verifies refusal without changing sentinel.\n- Exercise concurrent destination creation with overwrite false and ensure atomic failure; verify forced output replaces link entry without following it.\n- Integration-test a preplanted link at the legacy PID partial name; an unrelated file must remain unchanged.\n- Ensure staging files are exclusively created in a private random directory.\n- Retain documented final plan symlink resolution while refusing existing staging links.\n- Add deterministic bounded-input regression coverage around the broken control; for filesystem staging use isolated integration cases with pre-existing symlink entries and verify unrelated files remain unchanged.\n- Precreate the former PID-based temporary name as a symlink and verify its target and the original plan remain unchanged.\n- Verify safe staging preserves existing plan bytes on write or commit failure.\n\nPreventive controls:\n- Keep filesystem publication policy shared across native and binding CLIs."
          },
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "codexSecurity/v1": "codex-security/v1:sha256:489bf04a7af86031f7b656191e68d8665448245908ea9610d25cabcb86be5975"
